---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Sessions 25&ndash;26: Present Back &amp; Reflect</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">The Arcade Cabinet Project &mdash; Milestone 5 of 5</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <h2>Closing the Loop With Your Client</h2>
          <p>
            Milestone 1 started with a brief from a real or surrogate
            client. These two sessions close that loop &mdash; presenting
            what you built back to the person or team who asked for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            This also closes all of Unit 2 &mdash; eight weeks from "what is
            digital manufacturing" to a real, unique cabinet built for a
            real client.
          </p>

          <p>
            A team whose cabinet doesn't perfectly match their original
            vision, but who can clearly explain what changed and why,
            demonstrates the same competency as a team whose build went
            exactly to plan. Honest explanation matters more than a
            flawless outcome.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Prepare a presentation or written defense &mdash; check with
            your instructor which format &mdash; covering all five of these:
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>1. How This Answers Your Client's Brief</h3>
          <p>
            Reference your actual Milestone 1 requirements summary. Explain,
            specifically, how your finished cabinet addresses the User,
            Need, Constraints, and Success Criteria you documented back then
            &mdash; not a retroactive justification that sounds better now.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>2. Your Design Decisions</h3>
          <p>
            Explain your concept choice, your material choices, and your
            route-or-print decisions &mdash; and why, using your actual
            Milestone 2&ndash;3 reasoning, not vague preference.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>3. Your CAM Decisions</h3>
          <p>
            Pick <b class="text-sn-theme">one panel</b> and walk through how
            you programmed it: which toolpath types it needed and why, your
            offset choice, your step-over and step-down, and where you put
            tabs. Then explain one CAM decision you'd make differently now.
          </p>
          <p>
            Fusion was the biggest new skill in this unit &mdash; this is
            where you show you can reason about it, not just operate it.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>4. What Changed During the Build</h3>
          <p>
            State honestly what differed between your CAD model and your
            physical cabinet. For anything that changed, give a specific
            reason &mdash; a material behaving unexpectedly, a CAM setup
            issue, an assembly problem &mdash; not a vague "it needed some
            tweaks."
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>5. Your Documentation</h3>
          <p>
            Submit your full documentation trail: Milestone 1's requirements
            summary, Milestone 2's decision matrix and material choices,
            Milestone 3's CAM records, and Milestone 4's build log &mdash;
            showing the project's real evolution, not just a polished final
            summary.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you present or submit, confirm:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Your brief-alignment explanation references your actual original requirements, not a rewritten version</li>
            <li>Your CAM walkthrough covers a specific panel with real parameter choices &mdash; not a general description of how CAM works</li>
            <li>Every build change (if any) has a specific, named cause &mdash; not a vague explanation</li>
            <li>Your documentation is complete from Milestone 1 through today, not just a final-state summary</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these on your own. Reflections are individual, even when the rest of the session was team work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Your client gave you a brief back in Milestone 1. Knowing what you know now, what's the one question you wish you'd asked them then &mdash; and what would you have done differently with the answer?</li>
            <li>This is the last reflection of the course. What's one habit from this class &mdash; asking clarifying questions, checking a simulation before trusting it, diagnosing instead of guessing &mdash; you think you'll actually keep using outside of it?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Submit your presentation or written defense, and your complete
            project documentation.
          </p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Project Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
